# Database Rules (`packages/database/*`)

## Schema conventions

- Every model gets an `@@index` on foreign keys used in frequent lookups
  (already established for `societyId`, `categoryId`, `buyerId` — follow
  this pattern for any new relation).
- Use `cuid()` for IDs, not auto-incrementing integers — these IDs may be
  exposed in URLs (slugs aside) or shared externally, and sequential IDs
  leak volume information.
- Money fields are `Decimal`, never `Float` or `Int` — floating point
  rounding errors in tax/escrow calculations are a correctness bug, not a
  cosmetic one.
- Enums over free-text strings for any field with a closed, known set of
  values (status fields, plot types, roles).

## The audit ledger is append-only — enforce, don't just document

- `LedgerEvent` rows must never be updated or deleted at the application
  layer. When writing migrations, ensure the Postgres trigger from ADR-004
  blocking `UPDATE`/`DELETE` on this table is in place — don't rely on
  convention alone.
- If a new domain package produces a new kind of mutation (anything in
  booking/escrow/allocation/balloting), it must emit a `LedgerEvent` through
  the standard builder — don't write ad-hoc audit rows.

## Sensitive fields

- `cnicEncrypted` / `ntnEncrypted` are the only acceptable storage form for
  these fields. Never add a new plaintext CNIC/NTN column anywhere in the
  schema, including on join tables, denormalized views, or seed fixtures
  that might get committed.
- Encryption/decryption goes through `packages/database/encryption.ts`
  exclusively — don't inline `crypto` calls elsewhere.

## Migrations

- One logical change per migration. Don't bundle an unrelated schema change
  into a migration generated for a different feature.
- Run `prisma migrate dev` locally and check the generated SQL before
  committing — Prisma's diff can occasionally propose a destructive change
  (column drop/recreate) for what looks like a benign edit; catch this
  before it ships.

## Seed data

- Keep seed data realistic and Pakistani-context-appropriate (real city
  names, valid-format CNIC/NTN patterns, plausible PKR amounts) — this data
  doubles as documentation for what valid records look like.
- Never seed a `LedgerEvent` via direct insert bypassing the domain ledger
  builder — seed via the same code path production would use, so the seed
  script also exercises the real flow.
